<article class="offering-card">
  <div class="offering-photo" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/assets/images/suburbanwaterheater.png"></div>
  <div class="offering-body">
    <h2 id="waterheaterflush">Water Heater Flush</h2>
    <p>
      Over time, sediment and mineral deposits can collect in your RV&rsquo;s
      water heater tank. That buildup can reduce heating efficiency and shorten
      the life of the tank. I&rsquo;ll flush the tank and use water and compressed
      air to loosen and remove as much buildup as possible.
    </p>
    <h4>Anode Rod Inspection</h4>
    <p>
      If you have a Suburban tank-style water heater, I&rsquo;ll also check the
      anode rod while the tank is drained. The rod gradually wears away as it
      protects the steel tank from corrosion. Suburban recommends replacing it
      once a year, or sooner if more than 75% of the rod material has worn away.
      Once the rod is depleted, the tank is more vulnerable to corrosion.
      If your water heater needs a new anode rod, replacement is included in the service price.
    </p>
    <h4>Tank-Style Water Heaters vs. Tankless Water Heaters</h4>
    <p>
      This flushing service is for tank-style RV water heaters. Anode rod
      inspection applies to models equipped with one. Tankless water heaters
      have separate maintenance requirements that vary by model.
    </p>
    <div class="offering-price">
      <rv:price regular="169" discount="<%= GRAND_OPENING_PERCENT %>" />
    </div>
  </div>
</article>